<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="Learn more about the author and the purpose of this OSINT learning blog."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <h1>About this blog</h1>
    <p>I started this site to document my journey into open source intelligence (OSINT). Rather than keeping notes scattered across folders and documents, I wanted a public place to share what I learn, what I get wrong, and how I improve over time.</p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>What is OSINT?</h2>
    <p>Open source intelligence is the process of collecting and analyzing information from publicly available sources. That includes social media, public records, news articles, websites, maps, images, and anything else accessible without specialized access or illegal methods.</p>
    <h2>What you'll find here</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Walkthroughs of OSINT tools and techniques</li>
      <li>Reflections on investigations and practice challenges</li>
      <li>Notes on ethics, privacy, and operational security</li>
      <li>Resources that have helped me learn</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>About me</h2>
    <p>I'm a student of OSINT. I come from a cybersecurity and technology background and I'm building my skills in online research, digital footprint analysis, and investigation methodology. This blog is as much for my own learning as it is for anyone else on a similar path.</p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
